ate standards health and safety  standards for the dormitories for the  workers but again there was no real  framework or guidance for how to do this  again we're kind of creating it they're  based on the needs of that particular  project so from there I moved to London  and after a year doing some more  commercial analyst stuff so that you  know I didn't forget how to use XL but  anyway I rejoined our group vice  president for strategy and policy  development and after a few months  working for him he asked me to focus on  human rights and he admittedly didn't  know what that meant but there seemed to  be a lot going on with respect to  business and human rights so a number of  our projects in difficult places were  ramping up at the same time so the  project I was on in Indonesia was  finally starting major construction we  had a big similar project in  gola which was starting major  construction we were starting to see a  first oil pop out of this pipeline that  we were building across azerbaijan  turkey and georgia and all these  projects rightly we're attracting the  attention of human rights groups and  then so those that was sort of some  internal factors and externally there  seemed to be a lot going on about  business and human rights so there was a  debate over this proposed set of  standards going on at the UN which I'll  talk about in a second our industry  association had set up a human rights  task force there was something called  the business leaders initiative on Human  Rights which was picking up steam there  so they seem to be a lot going on so we  said basically go figure out what's  going on figure out if we need to be  doing anything differently so i spent  about nine months having lots of  conversations with our staff internally  in different parts of the world getting  up to speed on the external debate and  it didn't start out as you know produce  a policy but actually what people were  crying out for was a framework they said  you know we'r